## a b s t r a c t
In this work, we fabricated vanadium/zinc metal–organic frameworks (V/Zn-MOFs) derived from selfassembled metal organic frameworks, to further disperse ultrasmall $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanoparticles and encapsulate them in a nitrogen-doped nanocarbon network (ZVO/NC) under in situ pyrolysis. When employed as an anode for lithium-ion batteries, ZVO/NC delivers a high reversible capacity $\left(807\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}\right.$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\Omega}}.$ and excellent rate performance $372\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $8.0\mathsf{A g}^{-1}\underline{{\cdot}}$ . Meanwhile, when used in sodium-ion batteries, it exhibits long-term cycling stability (7000 cycles with $145\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $2.0\mathsf{A g}^{-1}\frac{\d H_{\mathsf{A}}}{\d t}.$ ). Additionally, when employed in potassium-ion batteries, it also shows outstanding electrochemical performance with reversible capacities of $264\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and $140\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ for 1000 cycles. The mechanism by which the pseudocapacitive behaviour of ZVO/NC enhances battery performance under a suitable electrolyte was probed, which offers useful enlightenment for the potential development of anodes of alkaliion batteries. The performance of $\mathrm{{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{{VO}_{4}}}$ as an anode for SIBs/PIBs was investigated for the first time. This work provides a new horizon in the design ZVO/NC as a promising anode material owing to the intrinsically synergic effects of mixed metal species and the multiple valence states of V.
