## 2.4. Electrochemical Measurements
For the LIB test, the working electrode was obtained by mixing the $Z{\mathsf{V O}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ or ZVO/ZO/NC samples, super-P and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone at a weight ratio of 8 : $1:1$ . The solvents eventually evaporated, and the electrode films were dried at $110^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum for 10 h. A 1 M solution of $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ in ethylene carbonate (EC)/dimethyl carbonate (DMC)/ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC) (1:1:1 by volume) served as the electrolyte. Lithium metal foil was used as the reference electrode, and Celgard 2320 was used as the separator. For SIB and PIB tests, the working electrodes were prepared following the same procedure with a mass ratio of the ZVO/NC or ZVO/ZO/NC samples, super-P, and carboxymethyl cellulose (CMC) of 8: 1: 1. Sodium or potassium metal foil was used as the reference electrode, and Whatman glass fibre was used as the separator. ${\mathrm{NaPF}}_{6}$ (1 M) including ethylene carbonate (EC)/dimethyl carbonate (DMC)/ ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC) (1:1:1 by volume) was used as the electrolyte for SIBs. ${\mathrm{KPF}}_{6}$ (1 M) was dissolved in ethylene carbonate (EC)/diethyl carbonate (DEC) (1:1 by volume) as the electrolyte for PIBs. The mass loading on each electrode for LIBs, SIBs and PIBs can be calculated with the value of $1.0{-}1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ , respectively. The galvanostatic charge–discharge curves and charge–discharge performance were tested with Land CT 2001A battery instruments. The cyclic voltammograms (CV; Ivium, Netherlands) were collected on an electrochemical workstation at room temperature in the voltage range $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out at frequency range of $10^{-1}\mathrm{Hz}$ to $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ with a voltage disturbance amplitude $1.0\mathrm{mV}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure and morphology characterization
Powder XRD measurements were utilized to characterize the patterns of all samples (Fig. 1a). The XRD pattern of $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z n-M O F}$ (the precursor) is shown in Fig. S1a. The precursor exhibits distinct crystals with cubic $I43{\mathrm{~m}}$ groups and strong diffraction peaks (labelled as $\"\bigcirc\boldsymbol{\Psi}^{\prime\prime}.$ ) at $2\theta$ values of 7.4, 10.4, 12.8, 14.7, 16.5, and $18.1^{\circ}$ , which match the (011), (002), (112), (022), (013) and (222) crystals of well-known ZIF-8 structures, respectively [45]. When elevating the calcination temperature to $500^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ the precursor $(\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{\Sigma}$ Zn-MOF) was converted in situ into a ZVO/ZO/NC composite. That the distinguishable peaks (labelled as $"\bullet\vartriangle$ ) located at 31.7, 34.3, and $36.2^{\circ}$ are ascribed to the (10 0), (0 02), and (101) planes of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (JCPDS No. 80-0074), respectively [46]. Then, with increasing calcination temperature, single phase, well-crystallized $\mathrm{{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{{VO}_{4}}}$ was obtained at $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The pattern of $Z{\mathsf{V O}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ is the same as that of ZVO/ZO/NC and no impurity peaks (ZnO) appear in the red pattern. The diffraction peaks of ZVO/NC become sharper, demonstrating the growth of crystallites and enhanced crystallinity. The strong diffraction peaks are located at 18.3, 30.1, 35.4, 37.1, 43.1, 53.4, 56.9 and $62.5^{\circ}$ , which correspond well to the (11 1), (22 0), (31 1), (40 0), (42 2) (51 1) and (44 0) crystal planes of standard cubic $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ (JCPDS No. 76-1632) [47]. A continuous increase in annealing temperature leads to the in situ formation of the $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ polyhedron composite with a strongly coupled carbon matrix. The broad yet distinguishable peaks of ZVO/ZO/NC and ZVO/NC at approximately $26^{\circ}$ indicate that the carbon matrixes derived from ZIF-8 are amorphous carbon structures. Fig. 1b shows the Raman spectra of the ZVO/ZO/NC and ZVO/NC composites. Broad D-band (disordered band) and G-band (graphite band) peaks are seen ca. 1360 and $1599~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . As the calcination temperature increases, the calculated value of the peak intensity ratio for ZVO/NC $\mathrm{(I_{D}/}$ $\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}=1.12)$ becomes larger than that of ZVO/ZO/NC $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}=1.08\right)$ , revealing the increase in carbon defects in the $Z{\mathsf{V O}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ structure. Abundant carbon defects, possibly introduced by N heteroatom doping, are beneficial for enhancing extra alkali ion storage sites [48]. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted to further characterize the carbon content within $Z V O/{\mathsf{N C}}$ in air (Fig. 1c). The weight loss of ZVO/NC (ca. $15\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ ) below $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ is because of the evaporation of adsorbed moisture. Based on a consecutive weight loss of carbon between ca. 200 and $700~^{\circ}C$ , the carbon and $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ contents of ZVO/NC can be calculated to be ca. 55 wt $\%$ and $45\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. The BET surface area and pore volume of $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z n-M O F}$ are calculated to be $1062~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.184~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. S1d). Through a Barrett-Joyner-Halendar (BJH) evaluation, the pore-size distribution is indicative of average mesopores of $2.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ within $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z n-M O F}$ , as shown in Fig. S1d (inset). The specific surface area of the ZVO/NC is $351~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with diameters centred at $3.2~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1d and inset). The larger specific surface area of the ZVO/NC polyhedron and abundant pore networks can shorten the ion transport distance and enhance the electrode/electrolyte contact area.
The elemental composition of the ZVO/NC polyhedron was further confirmed by XPS (Fig. 2). The XPS spectra on the ZVO/NC surface reveal the characteristic signals of Zn, V, O, C and N without impurities (Fig. 2a). In the $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ XPS spectrum (Fig. 2c), two obvious peaks are detected at 1021.7 and $1044.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding well to $\mathtt{Z n2p}_{3/2}$ and Zn $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ spin orbitals, respectively, confirming the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ state of Zn. For the high-resolution $\mathtt{V2p}$ spectra, there are three peaks of $\mathsf{V}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{V}^{4+}$ , and $\mathsf{V}^{5+}$ at 515.9, 517.1, and $517.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The primary peak was located at a binding energy of $517.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , implying the presence of $\mathsf{V}^{4+}$ (highlighted by a light blue colour) in $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ [49–51]. Likewise, as shown in Fig. 2d, four peaks of the O 1s spectrum are located at approximately 529.8, 530.5, 531.7 and $532.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which correspond to lattice oxygen, Zn-O and V-O, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (carbonate), and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{O}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ (carbonate) species, respectively [52]. In addition, defects and dopants are two important factors influencing the electrochemical performance [53]. As shown in Fig. 2e, the peaks at approximately 284.8, 285.3, 286.9 and $289.5\mathrm{eV}$ are associated with $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ , $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{=}\mathsf{N}$ , $c{-}0$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds, respectively. This validates the successful in situ N atom doping in the carbon matrix, which is consistent with the above results of Raman spectrum. The N 1s spectrum (Fig. 2f) are deconvolved into three types of nitrogen species located at 398.6, 400.2 and $401.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ which can be interpreted as the pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N, respectively. In detail, the presence of pyridinic N and pyrrolic N is believed to induce many highly chemically active sites at the edges or in the defect sites of the basal plane while graphitic N can enhance the conductivity and stability of nanocomposites [54–56]. As shown in Fig. 2g, the relative ratios of pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N are 49.4, 30.1, and $20.5\%$ , respectively. Additionally, according to the results of elemental analysis (Table S1), the carbon and nitrogen contents in $Z V O/N C$ ca. 35.13 and $10.65~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. N-doped activated carbon has been taken into account for the active sites for the electrochemical reaction process, which can not only improve the surface wettability between organic electrolytes and active materials, but also facilitate the adsorption and charge-transfer ability of $\mathrm{Li}/$ $\mathsf{N a}/\mathsf{K}$ ions, accelerating the pseudo-capacitance behaviour.
The architecture of ZVO/NC was analysed by SEM and TEM characterizations (Fig. 3). As depicted in Fig. S2a, V/Zn-MOF was initially prepared as a precursor to fabricate ZVO/NC. The precursors are monodispersed with sizes ranging between 200 and $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ and possess regular polyhedron morphology. The V/ZnMOF precursor was transformed into ZVO/ZO/NC via calcination at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ (Fig. S2b). The structure of the polyhedron is well maintained while the surface becomes rough after increasing the calcination temperature to $700^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. 3a-b). However, the structure of the polyhedron undergoes visible damage after increasing the calcination temperature to $800^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ under an Ar atmosphere (Fig. S2d), suggesting the importance of proper calcination temperature to the structure of the polyhedron. As shown in Fig. 3c, owing to the strong interaction between carbon layers derived from PVP and the rhomboid dodecahedron carbon skeleton derived from V/Zn-MOF, the overgrowth of $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanoparticles is restricted in the carbon matrix, leading to the formation of a sweetsop-like shell configuration. The dodecahedral configuration of ZVO/NC can be well preserved from $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{n}$ -MOF with a higher calcination temperature and the entire structures have a uniform size distribution of approximately $100{-}300~\mathrm{nm}$ , quite similar to that of $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z n-M O F}$ . TEM images show further insight into the interior architecture of the ZVO/NC polyhedron. The low-magnification TEM images (Fig. 3d-e) provide evidence of the scale-uniformity of the dodecahedra with similar thicknesses. Furthermore, Fig. 3e reveals numerous $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanoparticles with high crystallinity that are tightly and homogeneously confined within the carbon matrix. Lattice fringes of 0.242, 0.209 and $0.171\ \mathrm{nm}$ are well indexed to the (222), (400) and (422) lattice planes of $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}.$ respectively. The corresponding EDS mappings in Fig. 3f reveal the homogeneous distribution of Zn, V, O, C, and N, which demonstrates that all atoms are uniformly dispersed into the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon matrix.
## 3.2. Electrochemical properties
## 3.2.1. Electrochemical performance for LIBs
The electrochemical performances of ZVO/NC as the anode material of LIBs is shown in Fig. 4. In the CV curves for ZVO/NC at a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{~m~V~}\boldsymbol{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4a), two broad reduction peaks ca. $1.46~\mathrm{V}$ and $0.55~\mathrm{V}$ appeared, which correspond to the first lithium intercalation of $\mathrm{\Zn_{2}V O_{4}}$ to form $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{VO}_{2}$ and the conversation reaction to form ZnO in the initial cathodic cycle [57]. Moreover, a third sharp intense peak is found below $0.1\mathrm{~V~}$ and has asymmetrical features. This peak of the first reduction curve is larger than other curves below approximately $0.1\mathrm{\V}$ which are attributed to the consumption of part of the electrolyte, forming a solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) film [58]. The intense asymmetric peak below $0.1\mathrm{\V}$ arises from the overlapping of two peaks related to an irreversible $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ conversion reaction that results in the formation of Li-Zn alloy due to the further reduction of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ into Zn. In contrast, the initial anodic sweep displays three oxidation peaks: one weak peak at approximately $0.53\mathrm{V}$ (below 1.0 V) corresponds to delithiation process of the Li-Zn alloy [47]. The other two peaks at 1.22 and $2.62\mathrm{~V~}$ should be ascribed to oxidization of Zn to ZnO and extraction of Li ions from $\mathrm{Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{VO}_{2}$ matrices, accompanied by transformations of $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{V}^{3+}$ , and $\mathsf{V}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{V}^{4+}$ , respectively [59]. The lithiation and conversion reaction of $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ for LIBs may be expressed as follows [60,61]:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}+\mathrm{\xLi^{+}}+\mathrm{\xe^{-}}\rightarrow\mathrm{\mathrm{2ZnO}}+\mathrm{\Li}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{VO}_{2}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathrm{~\ZnO}+\mathrm{\2Li^{+}}+\mathrm{\mathrm{2e^{-}}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{\mathrm{Zn}}+\mathrm{\Li}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\end{array}
$$
$$
Z n+y\mathrm{Li^{+}+y e^{-}\rightarrow L i_{y}Z n}
$$
Fig. 4b displays that the initial discharge and charge capacities of the ZVO/NC electrode are 1111 and $619\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. The irreversible capacity loss at the 1st cycle can be ascribed to the formation of an SEI layer, which is consistent with the CV curve results. Additionally, the discharge capacities for the 2nd, 3rd, 50th, 100th and 200th cycles of ZVO/NC are 675, 662,
666, 786 and 807 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, indicating the good reversibility and capacitance stability of ZVO/NC. Fig. 4c shows the cycling performances of the ZVO/NC and ZVO/ZO/NC electrodes at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ for 200 cycles. ZVO/NC presents the an excellent initial capacity $1111\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ ) and maintains stable capacity of $807\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles. ZVO/NC delivers the best high-rate performance and benign capacity retention at large current densities as shown in Fig. 4d. The average reversible capacities of the ZVO/NC electrode are 658, 604, 532, 442, 397 and $372\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , at current densities of 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 6.0 and $8.0\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The ZVO/NC electrode even maintains a discharge capacity of 718 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the rate returns to $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The cycling capability of the ZVO/NC electrode $(441\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) was further evaluated at an ultrahigh rate of $5.0\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ with a long lifespan of 1000 cycles, as shown in Fig. 4e. The Coulombic efficiency of the ZVO/NC anode approaches to $100\%$ with a capacity retention approximately $83.2\%$ just after several cycles.
To further investigate the reason for the enhanced cycling stability and rate capability of ZVO/NC electrodes, CV curves from 0.2 to $10.0\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\ s^{-1}$ were produced (Fig. 5a). Specifically, the relationship of the measured peak current (i) and scan rate $(\nu)$ can be described by the following two equations [62,63]:
$$
i=\mathsf{a}\cdot\boldsymbol\nu^{\flat}
$$
$$
\log i={\mathsf{b}}\cdot\log\ \nu+\log\ {\mathsf{a}}
$$
where both $a$ and $b$ are controllable constant parameters. The value of factor b ranged from 0.5 (diffusion-controlled process) to 1.0 (capacitive process). In this case, the relationship between log i and log $\upsilon$ of the ZVO/NC electrodes (Fig. 5b), the calculated b values are $0.84\pm0.012$ (peak-A) and $0.81\pm0.013$ (peak-B), respectively, which means that ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ storage is influenced by both diffusion-controlled and pseudocapacitance processes. The capacitive contribution can be calculated from the following equation [64]:
$$
i_{(\upsilon)}=k_{1}\upsilon+k_{2}\cdot\upsilon^{1/2}
$$
where $i_{(v)},v,k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are the measured current, fixed potential V, scan rate, and adjustable constant parameters, respectively. As illustrated in Fig. 5c, the pseudocapacitive contribution reaches $43\%$ at $1.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . The capacitive contributions of the ZVO/NC electrodes gradually increase as the scan rate $v$ shifts (from 0.2 to $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ ), as shown in Fig. 5d. These results demonstrate that the existence of pseudocapacitive behaviour can effectively improve electrochemical performance, resulting in kinetics which facilitate storage of Li ions on the surface or in the near surface region, as well as creating superior long cycling performance and rate capability.
## 3.2.2. Electrochemical performance for SIBs
Considering the promising behaviour of the as the ZVO/NC anode for lithium storage, we further studied its capability in sodium ion batteries. To optimize the performance of the $Z\mathsf{V O}/$ NC electrode for SIBs, different electrolytes (ester-based or etherbased) were given priority for investigation [65]. Several factors deserve attention, such as the various Na-salts $(\mathsf{N a P F}_{6}$ , $\mathsf{N a C l O}_{4}$ or ${\mathrm{NaCF}}_{3}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3};$ ) and solvents including propylene carbonate (PC), ethylene carbonate (EC), diethyl carbonate (DEC), dimethyl carbonate (DMC), ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC), dimethoxyethane (DME)
and diethylene glycol dimethyl ether (DIGLYME) or mixtures thereof. To illustrate the significance of the electrolytes and corresponding solvents, the electrochemical behaviours of the ZVO/NC electrode using different electrolytes were also examined. As shown in Fig. 6a, when using $1.0\mathrm{~M~NaPF_{6}}$ in EC/DMC/EMC as the electrolyte, the ZVO/NC electrode exhibits the highest reversible capacity performance. As a contrast, when using 1.0 M $\mathsf{N a C l O}_{4}$ in EC/DMC/EMC or $1.0\mathrm{M}\mathrm{NaPF_{6}}$ in PC, the ZVO/NC electrode displays a lower capacity (ca. $170\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . On the other hand, compared with ester-type solvents (EC/DMC/EMC and PC), when using ether-type solvents including DME or DIGLYME, the $Z\mathsf{V O}/$ NC electrode suffers a sharp capacity decay to $<150\mathrm{\mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ or even less after 50 cycles. Consequently, the results have demonstrated that the cycling performance of $Z V O/{\mathsf{N C}}$ in ester-based electrolytes is superior to that in ether-based systems. This ternary mixture may be able to provide efficient ionic conductivity, low viscosity, low ion pairing, and, more significantly, the formation of opportune and stable SEI films. As shown in Fig. S3a, CV curves of ZVO/NC at $0.1~\mathrm{\mV}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ were obtained in the voltage range between 0.01 and $3.00\mathrm{~V~}$ (versus $\mathsf{N a}^{+}/\mathsf{N a})$ . For the initial cathodic cycle, the reduction peak of approximately $1.0\mathrm{V}$ is different from the subsequent scans likely due to the formation of the solid electrolyte SEI membrane. One broad anodic peak centre at $1.0\substack{-1.25\mathrm{V}}$ is assigned to the oxidation reaction. From the 2nd cycle onward, the CV curves substantially overlap, representing superior reversible ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion/extraction in ZVO/NC. The corresponding electrochemical performances are displayed in Fig. S3b. ZVO/NC delivers a stable reversible capacity of $266\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after the 100th cycle at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ in Fig. 6b. The ZVO/ZO/NC electrode exhibits an inferior capacity of $204\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the 100th cycle. Meanwhile, ZVO/NC displays fascinating reversible charge capacities of 368, 290, 255,
221, 177, 164 and $110\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 and 4.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which were much better than those of $\scriptstyle{Z\lor0/Z0/\Nu C}$ (Fig. S3c-d). As displayed in Fig. 6c, after enduring 1000 cycles at $1.0\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the maintained capacity and the Coulombic efficiency for ZVO/NC are $185\mathrm{~\mAh~\g^{-1}~}$ and close to $99\%$ respectively. It is especially notable that ZVO/NC demonstrates excellent ultralong cycling stability, delivering a capacity of 145 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $2.0\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ after the 7000th cycle, with a coulombic efficiency of almost $100\%$ (Fig. 6d).
CV measurements of the ZVO/NC anode for SIBs were carried out at a series of scan rates from $0.2\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ to $1.0\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 7a, as the scan rates increase, all the CV curves display similar shapes with a slight potential polarization, implying regular variation of different charge transfer kinetics. Fig. 7b shows that the $b$ values of the ZVO/NC anode are $0.89\pm0.004$ for the anodic peaks and $0.94\pm0.019$ for the cathodic peaks, suggesting that the sodium storage kinetics of the $Z{\mathsf{V O}}/{\mathsf{N C}}$ anode are also a mixture of pseudocapacitive behaviour and ion diffusion control. The detailed pseudocapacitive fraction of the ZVO/NC anode at $0.4~\mathrm{mV}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ is also illustrated in Fig. 7c. As shown in Fig. 7d, the pseudocapacitive contribution increases from $76\%$ to $93\%$ as the scan rate increases from $0.2\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ to $1.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . These results verify that the pseudocapacitive behaviour plays a key role in the entire capacity of the ZVO/NC anode. This might be attributed to the effect on the N atom doping in carbon, as there are more redox reactions on the surface of the carbon matrix.
## 3.2.3. Electrochemical performance for PIBs
In view of the remarkable performance of ZVO/NC in SIBs, PIBs were also assembled and tested. Recently, different electrolytes and organic solvents have been studied as for anode materials for PIBs, which is significant for enhancing the electrochemical performance of K-ion storage. In the literature, the most common electrolyte for PIBs is potassium hexafluorophosphate $(\mathrm{KPF}_{6})$ , which is dissolved in different organic solvents, similar to SIBs. As shown in Fig. 8a, we can observe that the capacity of the $Z\mathsf{V O}/$ NC anode in the EC/DEC electrolyte is higher than those when other organic solvents are used. Utilizing $1.0\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{KPF}_{6}$ in EC/DEC as the electrolyte, a stable SEI layer forms that can effectively protect against solvent penetration of the active materials to maintain stablity between the electrode and electrolyte interface, thereby achieving superior electrochemical performances for PIBs [66,67]. In Fig. 8b, the ZVO/NC electrode exhibits a superior discharge capacity of $264~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}$ , while the capacity of ZVO/ZO/NC is only $159\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after the 80th cycle at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . The ZVO/NC electrode offers reversible capacities of 280, 242, 198, 169, 139 and $110\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 0.1 to $4.0\:\mathsf{A}\:\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Even when the current density returns to $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , it still exhibits a capacity of 266 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, even at higher rate current of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , a discharge capacity of $140\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ remain at the 1000th cycle with a Coulombic efficiency of almost $100\%$ (Fig. 8c). The CV of the ZVO/ NC electrode recorded at $0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ within the voltage range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ (versus $\mathrm{K}/\mathrm{K}^{+})$ , is shown in Fig. S4a. There are no obvious redox pair peaks and just one well-defined peak centred ca. $0.95\mathrm{V}$ in the initial cathodic scan, which can be attributed to the formation of nonstoichiometric $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ -intercalated compounds, accompanied by the formation of a stable SEI layer on the electrode/electrolyte interface. After the subsequent anodic scan, the anodic peak is located at $1.37\mathrm{V}$ which may be due to the reversible extraction of K ions in the electrode. To further ascertain the potassium-ion insertion/extraction reaction kinetics of the ZVO/NC electrode, additional CV measurements were further carried out ranging from 0.2 to $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\:\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , as shown in Fig. 8d. The b values could be individually quantified as $0.78\pm0.011$ (cathodic peak) and $0.83\pm0.010$ (anodic peak), which indicates that the reaction dynamics of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ are mainly dominated by a surface induced pseudocapacitance behaviour, Such behaviour suggests rapid ionic migration during the $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ insertion/extraction process, as shown in Fig. 8e. Fig. 8f shows that the ratio of capacitive behaviour is approximately $67\%$ for the specific voltage curve at $0.6\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , in which the pink shaded domain represents the dominant capacitive contribution in the whole region. Fig. $\mathbf{8g}$ depicts the histograms of the two processes at diverse scan rates. Distinctly, as the sweep rate of $v$ augments, the capacitive contribution of the ZVO/NC electrode gradually enlarges from $52\%$ to $63\%$ , then $67\%$ , then $73\%$ and finally to $77\%$ . Such a high capacitive contribution can presumably be attributed to the synergistic effect of the uniformly distributed $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanoparticles and superior conductivity of the carbon matrix.
In addition, to comprehensively understand the enhanced electrochemical behaviour of ZVO/NC, we employed electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) to characterize the electrodes before and after the 5th cycle. The Nyquist plots (Fig. 9) consist of three parts: the intercept is located at the $x$ -axis, the depressed semicircle appears at the high-medium frequency and a slope line presents at low frequency, corresponding to the internal resistance (Rs), the impedance of the SEI layer $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{f}})$ and the charge-transfer impedance of the electrode/electrolyte interface (Rct), and the semi-infinite diffusion of ions [68–72]. According to the equivalent circuit, the special value of each impedance is listed in Table S2. The Rf values of the fresh cells were 39.7, 127.7, and $230.5~\Omega$ in LIBs, SIBs and PIBs, respectively. The ${\sf R}_{\mathrm{f}}$ increased to 46.1, 159.3, and $_{373.8\ \Omega}$ after 5 cycles. The comparison of ZVO/NC to other vanadates (Table S3) and traditional materials (Table S4) for storage performance in the field of alkali ion batteries as reported in previous literature is provided. Despite direct statistical comparisons, zinc vanadate materials are suitable as anodes for LIBs/SIBs and exhibit decent electrochemical performance. As an anode material for sodium ion batteries, such materials are rarely able to exhibit excellent stability in long cycles $>5000$ cycles). Of particular note is the superior electrochemical storage performance of ZVO/NC including the superior reversible capacity $(807~\mathrm{\mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ ) for LIBs, the ultralong cycling performance (145 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 7000 cycles at $2.0\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ ) for SIBs, and the excellent cycle stability $140\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles at $0.5\mathsf{A}$ $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ for PIBs. This performance is superior to some representative vanadate anode materials, which should be ascribed to the meticulously tailored polyhedron with an $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous matrix and accessible active sites of ZVO/NC, favour improved electrode/electrolyte interfacial exchange of alkali ions and speedy electron trafficking. This shows that ZVO/NC shows electrochemical performance comparable to those of previous reports.
As demonstrated in Scheme 2, the outstanding $\mathrm{Li^{+}/N a^{+}/K^{+}}$ storage performance of ZVO/NC can be ascribed to the following characteristics: (i) Each individual polyhedron structure $100{-}300~\mathrm{nm};$ 1 behaves as a stable nanoreactor and isolation chamber forming an effective physical barrier and providing heterogeneous-phase confinement for dispersed $\mathrm{\Zn_{2}V O_{4}}$ nanoparticles. This helps to avoid excessive agglomeration of nanoparticles and ensures better ionic transport kinetics [73]. (ii) The porous characteristics and larger reasonable specific surface area $(351~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ of the carbon matrix not only ensure sufficient electrolyte/electrode contact, but also offer expanded space to serve as a cushion for volumetric variation in the repeated charge–discharge processes. (iii) The nitrogendoped nanostructure supplies more active sites for storing $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/\protect$ $\mathrm{Na^{+}/K^{+}}$ , and provides a vital pseudocapacitance effect [74]. As shown in Fig. S5, the ZVO/NC electrode maintains its polyhedral structure without a visible large range of aggregations after cycling for 50 cycles, while the ZVO/ZO/NC electrode shows large aggregations and obvious pulverization, indicating the high structural stability of the ZVO/NC electrode [75]. This novel architecture and suitable electrolyte surmount the depressed reaction kinetics and relieve the volume changes that occur during the electrochemical process in LIBs/SIBs, and even in PIBs.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a self-assembly strategy followed by in situ pyrolysis was developed to fabricate $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ nanoparticles encapsulated in the an N-doped nanocarbon network, which were derived from a $\mathsf{V}/\mathsf{Z n-M O F}$ precursor. Compared to previous higher-costly strategies (such as solvothermal method) to synthesize bimetallic vanadate material [30,33], we utilized a convenient ZIF-8 self-assembly route, placing V species into suitable cavities to form zinc vanadate polyhedron. The adopted $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -doped carbon encapsulated $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{VO}_{4}$ polyhedron, which display a uniform size distribution of approximately $100{-}300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and a larger specific surface area $(\Bar{3}\Bar{5}1~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), tends to improve the diffusion kinetics, favors enhanced electrochemical behaviour. When evaluated as an anode material for alkali ion batteries, ZVO/NC exhibits superior electrochemical performance. In particular, it delivers a superior reversible capacity $(807~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}.$ ) for LIBs, and presents an ultralong cycling performance (145 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 7000 cycles at $2.0\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}\dot{},$ ) for SIBs. It also shows excellent cycle stability (140 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ ) for PIBs. We also investigated different electrolytes for SIBs and PIBs, which provided suggestions for bimetallic oxide electrodes. Inspired by such strategy, series of advanced bimetallic oxide electrodes can be tailored and applied for other energy storage fields such as Li-S batteries, aqueous Zn-ion batteries, and supercapacitors.
